    "How weight changes the calories",
    "<p>Heavier bodies use more energy at the same speed, so a pad display that does not ask for your weight is guessing. The ACSM walking equation is the standard way to estimate this. The figures below are estimates, include resting energy, and assume a flat belt.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Body weight</th><th>2 mph, per hour</th><th>3 mph, per hour</th><th>3 mph at 5% incline, per hour</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>150 lb</td><td>181 kcal</td><td>236 kcal</td><td>384 kcal</td></tr>\n<tr><td>200 lb</td><td>241 kcal</td><td>314 kcal</td><td>511 kcal</td></tr>\n<tr><td>250 lb</td><td>302 kcal</td><td>393 kcal</td><td>639 kcal</td></tr>\n<tr><td>300 lb</td><td>362 kcal</td><td>471 kcal</td><td>767 kcal</td></tr>\n<tr><td>350 lb</td><td>422 kcal</td><td>550 kcal</td><td>895 kcal</td></tr>\n<tr><td>400 lb</td><td>483 kcal</td><td>629 kcal</td><td>1,023 kcal</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The equation is validated for roughly 1.9 to 3.7 mph, which covers normal pad speeds. To see only the extra energy from walking, subtract about one MET, roughly 1 kcal per kilogram per hour, from the figure.</p>"
